,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,37175,279,4771,315,26107,59,37018,45340,61,18,6558,61,17,37892,15170,21,87,61,17,12,24,87,92,76466,10061,400,64,3,387,279,1372,315,19766,304,279,4771,11,400,65,3,387,279,1372,315,12140,438,88,1307,6295,11,400,66,3,387,279,1372,315,16202,96254,6295,11,323,400,67,3,387,279,1372,315,1508,36426,96254,6295,13,7379,400,64,10,17,65,10,18,66,10,19,67,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nConsider the graph of $$\\frac{x^3-x^2+x}{6x^2-9x}.$$Let $a$ be the number of holes in the graph, $b$ be the number of vertical asympotes, $c$ be the number of horizontal asymptotes, and $d$ be the number of oblique asymptotes. Find $a+2b+3c+4d$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"95c905b59d62f43d9d3fe7dba9df18ea5eefbbfa6be9a253d7b85e1009e8fecc"},"seconds":46.13907814025879,"seed":716029084812543178,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given problem, we start by analyzing the function \\(\\frac{x^3 - x^2 + x}{6x^2 - 9x}\\).\n\nFirst, we simplify the function by factoring both the numerator and the denominator.\n\nThe numerator is:\n\\[ x^3 - x^2 + x = x(x^2 - x + 1) \\]\n\nThe denominator is:\n\\[ 6x^2 - 9x = 3x(2x - 3) \\]\n\nThus, the function can be rewritten as:\n\\[ \\frac{x(x^2 - x + 1)}{3x(2x - 3)} \\]\n\nWe can cancel the common factor of \\(x\\) from the numerator and the denominator, provided \\(x \\neq 0\\):\n\\[ \\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)} \\]\n\nNow, we analyze the simplified function \\(\\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)}\\).\n\n### Holes in the Graph\nA hole in the graph occurs where the function is undefined due to a common factor in the numerator and the denominator. In this case, the factor \\(x\\) was canceled, indicating a hole at \\(x = 0\\).\n\nThus, \\(a = 1\\).\n\n### Vertical Asymptotes\nVertical asymptotes occur where the denominator is zero and the numerator is non-zero. Setting the denominator \\(3(2x - 3) = 0\\), we find:\n\\[ 2x - 3 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nThus, there is one vertical asymptote at \\(x = \\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nThus, \\(b = 1\\).\n\n### Horizontal Asymptotes\nTo find horizontal asymptotes, we compare the degrees of the numerator and the denominator of the simplified function \\(\\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)}\\).\n\nThe degree of the numerator is 2, and the degree of the denominator is 1. Since the degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator, there is no horizontal asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(c = 0\\).\n\n### Oblique Asymptotes\nOblique asymptotes occur when the degree of the numerator is exactly one more than the degree of the denominator. Here, the degree of the numerator is 2 and the degree of the denominator is 1, so we perform polynomial long division to find the oblique asymptote.\n\nDividing \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(2x - 3\\), we get:\n\\[ \\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{2x - 3} = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{7}{4(2x - 3)} \\]\n\nThe quotient \\(\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{4}\\) represents the oblique asymptote.\n\nThus, \\(d = 1\\).\n\n### Final Calculation\nWe now calculate \\(a + 2b + 3c + 4d\\):\n\\[ a + 2b + 3c + 4d = 1 + 2(1) + 3(0) + 4(1) = 1 + 2 + 0 + 4 = 7 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{7}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
